<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">24 March in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 24 March, from 1401 to 1976.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1401 CE <span class="tih-ago">625 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Timur sacks Damascus</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Central Asian conqueror's army plundered and burned Damascus after its surrender, and its craftsmen were carried off to Samarkand. The Mamluk Sultanate survived, but Syria was devastated.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1603 CE <span class="tih-ago">423 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Elizabeth I dies and James VI of Scotland inherits England</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Elizabeth died at Richmond Palace, and her cousin James VI became James I of England and Ireland. The crowns were united, though the kingdoms stayed separate until 1707.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1765 CE <span class="tih-ago">261 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Parliament passes the Quartering Act</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The act required American colonies to provide barracks and supplies for British troops. Resentment of it was later listed among the grievances in the Declaration of Independence.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1882 CE <span class="tih-ago">144 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Robert Koch identifies the tuberculosis bacterium</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In a lecture in Berlin, Koch showed that tuberculosis, then causing about one in seven deaths in Europe, was caused by a bacterium. 24 March is now World Tuberculosis Day.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1944 CE <span class="tih-ago">82 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Allied prisoners break out in the 'Great Escape'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">That night 76 airmen crawled through a tunnel out of Stalag Luft III in Sagan. Only three reached safety; on Hitler's orders, 50 of those recaptured were shot.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/argentina" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/argentina.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1976 CE <span class="tih-ago">50 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Military coup overthrows Isabel Perón in Argentina</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A junta led by General Jorge Videla took power and began the 'Dirty War'. Thousands of people, estimated at up to 30,000, were 'disappeared' before military rule ended in 1983.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a></div></div></li></ol>
